<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Banjarmasin War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Banjar War · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin">Banjarmasin</a> War was a conflict in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonial government and supporters of the Banjar Sultanate, beginning in 1859 after the Dutch imposed their own candidate in a succession dispute following <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Adam's death. Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-antasari">Pangeran Antasari</a>, the resistance attacked Dutch posts and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> mines. The Dutch abolished the sultanate in 1860, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> fighting continued until 1905.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/banjarmasin-war.jpg" alt="Banjarmasin War" width="320" height="227" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W.A. van Rees · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1._ZM_Stoomschip_Celebes_in_gevecht_met_een_Kota_Mara_6_aug_1859_Poeloe_Kananat_opgenomen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borneo">Borneo</a> dated from 1606, and in 1635 the first contract was signed with the Sultanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/banjarmasin">Banjarmasin</a> for the supply of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a>. Disputes over unfulfilled contracts led to clashes, the worst in 1638, when 64 Dutch and 21 of their Japanese partners were killed at Kota Waring. After the British interlude of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a>, the Dutch returned in December 1816, signed a new contract with the sultan and replaced his flag with theirs in January 1817, and the Dutch Resident took more and more of the real power.</p><p>In 1852 the heir-apparent died and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> named the illegitimate grandson Tamjid in his place. Sultan Adam and many nobles appealed in vain for Hidayat, a younger but legitimate son, and under pressure from a Dutch warship sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martapura">Martapura</a> in 1856 Adam accepted Tamjid, with Hidayat as governor. Adam died in November 1857 and Tamjid succeeded him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In early 1859 a revolt broke out east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martapura">Martapura</a>. Hidayat, sent to suppress it, found a letter sealed by Tamjid urging the rebels to cause trouble, and resigned. Colonel Augustus Johannes Andresen took military command at Bandjermasin on 29 April 1859. On 1 May the Europeans at the Julia Hermina coal mine at Kalangan were killed, among them the Englishman James Motley, his wife and three children. Tamjid abdicated in June, and with no candidate left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> abolished the sultanate and ruled the territory directly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batavia">Batavia</a>, declaring Hidayat a renegade.</p><p>Through 1859 and 1860 the rebels under Demang Lehman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-antasari">Pangeran Antasari</a> and others fought at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martapura">Martapura</a>, Fort Tabanio, the Bumi Selamat Palace and Gunung Lawak, and attacked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> fort at Amawang on 31 March 1860.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>There is little evidence that Hidayat himself fought, though the war was often said to be waged in his name. He surrendered in early 1862 and lived out his days in Cianjur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-java">West Java</a> on a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> pension of 1,000 guilders a month. Antasari, a distant descendant of an earlier ruler who sought to restore the sultanate under himself, suffered a series of defeats by Major Govert Verspyck and died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> on 11 October 1862. The Dutch had won the main war by 1863, but sporadic fighting went on for decades. At the Battle of Baras Kuning Fort in 1905 the resistance leader Muhammad Seman was killed defending the fort.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Banjarmasin War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Expeditions against Bali in 1846, 1848 and 1849, the <b>Banjarmasin War</b> from 1859 and campaigns in Sulawesi and Borneo extended Dutch power step by step.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>A succession dispute after Sultan Adam's death led to the <b>Banjarmasin War</b> from 1859, led by Pangeran Antasari, and the Dutch abolished the sultanate in 1860, though resistance continued until 1905.</blockquote><a href="/state/banjar-sultanate">Banjar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
